  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Cuánto cuesta",
    "@en": "How much it costs"
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**얼마예요?** = ¿cuánto cuesta? **이거 얼마예요?** = ¿cuánto cuesta esto?",
     "@en": "**얼마예요?** = how much does it cost? **이거 얼마예요?** = how much is this?"
    },
    {
     "@es": "La moneda es el **원** (won) y los precios se dicen con los números de origen chino: 천 원, 오천 원.",
     "@en": "The currency is the **원** (won) and prices are said with Chinese origin numbers: 천 원, 오천 원."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Para decir «cada uno»: **한 개에** 천 원이에요 = a mil won la unidad.",
     "@en": "To say «cada uno»: **한 개에** 천 원이에요 = a thousand won the unity."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": {
    "@es": "Cien, mil, diez mil",
    "@en": "One hundred, one thousand, ten thousand."
   },
   "texto": [
    {
     "@es": "**백** = 100, **천** = 1 000 y **만** = 10 000. El «uno» de delante no se dice: mil es 천, no 일천.",
     "@en": "**백** = 100, **천** = 1 000 and **만** = 10 000. The «uno» from the front it is not said: a thousand is 천No 일천."
    },
    {
     "@es": "La gran diferencia con el español: el coreano no cuenta de mil en mil, sino de **diez mil en diez mil**. 20 000 no es «veinte mil», sino **이만** («dos diez-miles»). 50 000 = **오만**.",
     "@en": "The big difference with Spanish: the Korean does not count from a thousand to a thousand, but from **diez mil en diez mil**. 20 000 is not «Twenty thousand», but **이만** («two tiez- thousands»). 50 000 = **오만**."
    },
    {
     "@es": "Los precios en won tienen muchos ceros: un café ronda los 4 000 o 5 000 won, así que 만 se usa a todas horas.",
     "@en": "The prices in won have many zeros: a coffee around 4 000 or 5 000 won, so 만 is used all the time."
    }
   ]
  },
